00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  northern ireland's ports where the  problems of brexit are still playing out  now the uk government taking action to  unilaterally move most checks on trade  in goods from great britain to northern  ireland rowing back on a treaty long  signed with the eu  presentation of bill  northern ireland protocol bill this  legislation rips up part of the brexit  deal boris johnson signed in 2020 in  four key ways  it wants to create a check-free green  lane for goods that enter northern  ireland and don't go onto the republic  of ireland which is part of the european  union a dual regulatory regime meaning  goods could be sold in northern ireland  if they meet either uk or eu standards  new freedoms to provide financial  assistance to companies in northern  ireland and change vat rates  and it wants the european courts to stop  having a final say in disputes how  comfortable are you with the uk being  seen around the world as a rule breaker  instead of a rule enforcer  we're very clear that we're acting in  line with the law what is vitally  important  is that we do resolve this situation in  northern ireland that is causing real  problems the eu have refused to change  the protocol which is causing these  problems on trade on tax  and more broadly in northern ireland  but the eu say you signed it stick to it  and warns of retaliation the european  union will not renegotiate the protocol  unilateral action is damaging to mutual  trust  the commission will now assess the uk  draft legislation as the first step the  commission will consider continuing the  infringement procedure  a message reiterated by one key european  nation we are  forced because of uk action  to to respond to  what we certainly see as a breach of  international law i don't think there's  any other way to describe this if you  are legislating to set aside elements of  a of an international treaty which is  international law  uh well then you're breaking  international law  and north of the border unhappiness too  a majority of the northern ireland  assembly signing a joint letter to boris  johnson opposing what they say is a  reckless bill today's action by boris  johnson in westminster is absolutely  reckless it's disgraceful  it does nothing to serve the interests  of the people here it flies in the face  of an international agreement which he  himself negotiated it is in clear breach  of international law but for the dup  which won't support power sharing in the  northern ireland assembly until these  problems are fixed tentative support i  believe that  finally we are now seeing the kind of  action that is required  to begin the process  of  removing the barriers for trade within  the united kingdom  to restoring northern ireland's place  within the uk internal market for two  years now problems over trade between  london and brussels have been playing to  see but the solution proposed today only  serves to further stoke those divisions  with boris johnson pleasing his  brexiteers and the dup while angering  many in his own party brussels dublin  and washington dc many on both sides of  the argument will privately tell you  they don't think that the uk will in the  end enact this legislation that this is  a negotiating technique but it's one two  that will sour relations with old allies  on both sides of the atlantic and could  even eventually trigger a trade war with  the eu  that's an outcome some way off and one  no one wants but this trade route a  reminder that for all of the pm's  promises brexit's still not done beth  rigby sky news westminster

